Then a git show 0da11bf0cab90

  commit 0da11bf0cab9029db8b85e48d962ff05c00a4faa
  Author: Eiichi Tsukata <eiichi.tsukata@nutanix.com>
  Date:   Fri May 27 00:53:45 2022 +0000

    cpuidle: haltpoll: Add trace points for guest_halt_poll_ns grow/shrink

    Add trace points as are implemented in KVM host halt polling.
    This helps tune guest halt polling params.

    Signed-off-by: Eiichi Tsukata <eiichi.tsukata@nutanix.com>
    Acked-by: Marcelo Tosatti <mtosatti@redhat.com>
    Signed-off-by: Rafael J. Wysocki <rafael.j.wysocki@intel.com>

Which simply added the tracepoint without the guard.

I would then create the patch:

From: Steven Rostedt <rostedt@goodmis.org>
Subject: [PATCH] cpuidle: haltpoll: Only define guest_halt_poll_ns tracepoint
 when used

Tracepoints that are defined take up around 5K each, even if they are not
used. When a tracepoint is used only in code when a specific config is
enabled, their definition must also be only defined when the same config
is enabled otherwise it wastes memory.

The tracepoint guest_halt_poll_ns is only used when CONFIG_CPU_IDLE_GOV_HALTPOLL
is enabled. Guard the definition of that tracepoint with the same config.

Fixes: 0da11bf0cab90 ("cpuidle: haltpoll: Add trace points for guest_halt_poll_ns grow/shrink")
Signed-off-by: Steven Rostedt <rostedt@goodmis.org>
---
 include/trace/events/power.h | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/include/trace/events/power.h b/include/trace/events/power.h
index 370f8df2fdb4..4d17eb307b6a 100644
--- a/include/trace/events/power.h
+++ b/include/trace/events/power.h
@@ -497,6 +497,7 @@ DEFINE_EVENT(dev_pm_qos_request, dev_pm_qos_remove_request,
 	TP_ARGS(name, type, new_value)
 );

+#ifdef CONFIG_CPU_IDLE_GOV_HALTPOLL
 TRACE_EVENT(guest_halt_poll_ns,

 	TP_PROTO(bool grow, unsigned int new, unsigned int old),
@@ -525,6 +526,8 @@ TRACE_EVENT(guest_halt_poll_ns,
 	trace_guest_halt_poll_ns(true, new, old)
 #define trace_guest_halt_poll_ns_shrink(new, old) \
 	trace_guest_halt_poll_ns(false, new, old)
+#endif /* CONFIG_CPU_IDLE_GOV_HALTPOLL */
+
 #endif /* _TRACE_POWER_H */

 /* This part must be outside protection */
-- 
2.53.0

This is just one example, and this was an easy one. The guard was pretty
obvious. The event was added without the guard and the fix is simply to add
it.

Others could be more complex, where code was updated and moved. Or guards
were added, etc. But the main work is searching git via git blame and git
show. One trick to let people know is where you may need to look at git
history beyond a change because the change covers up what happened. If, for
example, the commit I found only changed the tracepoint logic but wasn't
the cause of the tracepoint being not found, I would have run:

  git blame 0da11bf0cab90~1 include/trace/events/power.h

Or done that on the code instead of the header file. The "~1" shows what
the code looked like *before* that commit. And gives the blame commits to
see what changed it.

